# CP-SAT User Notes

This file is **user-maintained**. The `cp-sat-scheduler` agent reads it at startup but never modifies it. Use it to record:

- Problem domain context the agent should know.
- Past decisions you want preserved across sessions.
- Conventions specific to this project (naming, granularity choices, etc.).
- Things the agent should always check or always avoid for your problem.

Delete the sections you don't need; add sections as your project evolves. The file is just markdown — structure it however helps you.

---

## Problem domain

(e.g., "Scheduling technician visits to customer sites. Each visit has a 1-hour window, technicians have skill requirements, vehicles have capacity for parts. Optimize for total travel time within 99% SLA on visit completion.")

## Decisions already made

(e.g., "Time granularity is 5 minutes — established because all visit durations are multiples of 5. Don't propose finer granularity unless we change the underlying data.")

## Project conventions

(e.g., "All task variable names follow `visit_{site_id}` pattern. Resource variables follow `tech_{tech_id}` pattern. Please follow these naming conventions in new code.")

## Things to always check

(e.g., "Always confirm the SLA constraint is satisfied before reporting a solution as feasible. Always sanity-check that travel times match the latest distance matrix in `data/distances.csv`.")

## Things to avoid

(e.g., "Don't propose decomposition by region until we have validated that monolithic doesn't scale; previous attempts at regional decomposition produced worse solutions due to cross-region rebalancing constraints.")

## Open questions for the agent to flag

(e.g., "We are unsure whether overtime should be a hard limit or a soft penalty. If the model makes this choice implicitly, please surface the assumption explicitly.")
